<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Takht-i-Bahi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Takht-i-Bahi monastery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Takht-i-Bahi is a Buddhist monastic complex on a hilltop near Mardan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, founded in the 1st century CE and used until about the 7th century. Its courts, stupas and meditation cells are among the best preserved in ancient Gandhara. An inscription found there, dated to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondophares">Gondophares</a>, helps fix Indo-Parthian chronology. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/takht-i-bahi.jpg" alt="Takht-i-Bahi" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asif Nawaz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takht-e-bahi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
